    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is this a good offer?\n\n**Yes, this is an exceptional, top-of-market offer for a Google L6 Product Manager.** \n\nTo put it into perspective:\n* **Market Benchmarks:** The median total compensation for a Google L6 PM in the Bay Area typically sits between **$525,000 and $580,000/year**.\n* **Your Offer's True Value:** Your 4-year average annual compensation is **~$697,000/year** (or **~$647,000/year** excluding the sign-on bonus). This places your offer firmly in the **90th+ percentile** for L6 PMs.\n* **Component Strength:**\n  * **Base Salary ($260,000):** Near the very top of Google\u2019s posted band ($183,000 \u2013 $271,000).\n  * **New-Hire GSUs ($1,340,000):** Far above the typical L6 starting grant ($800k\u2013$1M).\n  * **Sign-on Bonus ($200,000):** Extremely generous for L6 (standard is usually $50k\u2013$100k).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Breakdown: Meta IC6 vs. Google L6\n\nGoogle typically utilizes a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for GSUs (38% in Year 1, 32% in Year 2, 20% in Year 3, 10% in Year 4, or 33/33/22/12%). \n\nAssuming standard 38/32/20/10 vesting, here is how your cash flow compares to Meta:\n\n| Component | Current Meta Comp | Google Year 1 | Google Year 2 | Google Year 3 | Google Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $52,000 (20%) | $52,000 (20%) | $52,000 (20%) | $52,000 (20%) |\n| **Initial GSU Vest** | ~$250,000 | $509,200 (38%) | $428,800 (32%) | $268,000 (20%) | $134,000 (10%) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | \u2014 | $200,000 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n| **Annual Refresher Vest** | \u2014 | \u2014 | + Stacked Vest | + Stacked Vest | + Stacked Vest |\n| **Total Target Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$1,021,200** | **~$740,800** | **~$580,000+** | **~$446,000+** |\n\n*(Even under a straight 25%/year vesting schedule, Year 1 is **~$847,000** and annual average is **~$697,000**).*\n\n#### Key Takeaway on Equity & Refreshes:\nGoogle front-loads equity to provide a massive pay surge in Years 1 and 2. The recruiter\u2019s note about annual refreshers is standard: starting in Year 1, your performance-based refresh grants will begin vesting over subsequent years, which is designed to cushion the drop-off in Years 3 and 4.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n* **Walk Away?** Absolutely not. Google is your top choice, you are excited about the team, and the pay is a massive jump over your current comp.\n* **Aggressively Negotiate with Demands?** No. Google\u2019s compensation committee typically requires written competing offer letters to authorize significant increases. Without competing leverage, aggressive pushing can risk sounding misaligned when they have already presented a top-band offer.\n* **Make a Focused \"Closing Ask\"?** **Yes.** It is completely standard to make one polite, non-confrontational ask to optimize the package, tied directly to a commitment to sign immediately.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Ask For & How Much\n\nBecause your Base is already at ~96% of the posted band ceiling and your Sign-on is already very high, your best levers are a **slight equity bump** or a **modest sign-on adjustment**.\n\n#### The Target Request:\n* **Equity:** Ask to round up from **$1.34M to $1.45M \u2013 $1.50M** over 4 years.\n* **Alternative (Sign-on):** If they cannot move on equity, ask if they can increase the sign-on bonus to **$225,000 \u2013 $250,000**.\n\n#### Negotiation Script:\nYou can deliver this over the phone or via email:\n\n> *\"Thank you so much for putting this together. I\u2019m genuinely excited about the role and the team\u2014Google is my absolute first choice, and I\u2019m ready to commit to this team for the long haul.*\n> \n> *The offer is very strong. Given the unvested equity I\u2019m walking away from at Meta and the scope of this L6 role, if we can bridge the equity grant to **$1.45M** (or alternatively, increase the sign-on bonus to **$230k**), I am ready to sign the offer letter today without hesitation.*\n> \n> *Is that something we can take to the compensation committee?\"*\n\n### Bottom Line\nIf Google says yes, you secure an extra $30k\u2013$110k. If they say no and hold firm, you still hold an offer that pays significantly more than your current Meta comp at your dream company\u2014and you can sign with total confidence.",
